{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n \"ratio|min_width|no_wrap|overflow|expand\" docs/source'","aggregated_output":"docs/source/text.rst:48:- ``overflow`` should be \"fold\", \"crop\", or \"ellipsis\", and will override default overflow.\ndocs/source/text.rst:49:- ``no_wrap`` prevents wrapping if the text is longer then the available width.\ndocs/source/tables.rst:15:    table.add_column(\"Released\", justify=\"right\", style=\"cyan\", no_wrap=True)\ndocs/source/tables.rst:56:- ``min_width`` Sets a minimum width for the table.\ndocs/source/tables.rst:62:- ``expand`` Set to True to expand the table to the full available size.\ndocs/source/tables.rst:91:The :class:`~rich.table.Table` class offers a number of configuration options to set the look and feel of the table, including how borders are rendered and the style and alignment of the columns.\ndocs/source/tables.rst:141:- ``min_width`` When set to an integer will prevent the column from shrinking below this amount.\ndocs/source/tables.rst:143:- ``ratio`` Defines a ratio to set the column width. For instance, if there are 3 columns with a total of 6 ratio, and ``ratio=2`` then the column will be a third of the available size.\ndocs/source/tables.rst:144:- ``no_wrap`` Set to True to prevent this column from wrapping.\ndocs/source/tables.rst:166:    grid = Table.grid(expand=True)\ndocs/source/pretty.rst:28:Rich is quite conservative about expanding data structures and will try to fit as much in each line as it can. If you prefer, you can tell Rich to fully expand all data structures by setting ``expand_all=True``. Here's an example::\ndocs/source/pretty.rst:30:    >>> pprint([\"eggs\", \"ham\"], expand_all=True)\ndocs/source/introduction.rst:18:    PyCharm users will need to enable \"emulate terminal\" in output console option in run/debug configuration to see styled output.\ndocs/source/introduction.rst:36:To verify that Rich is installed correctly and to see a quick demonstration of its features, run the following command in your terminal::\ndocs/source/introduction.rst:102:`IPython Configuration <https://ipython.readthedocs.io/en/stable/config/intro.html>`_.\ndocs/source/columns.rst:18:        columns = Columns(directory, equal=True, expand=True)\ndocs/source/padding.rst:20:The Padding class can also accept a ``style`` argument which applies a style to the padding and contents, and an ``expand`` switch which can be set to False to prevent the padding from extending to the full width of the terminal. Here's an example which demonstrates both these arguments::\ndocs/source/padding.rst:24:    test = Padding(\"Hello\", (2, 4), style=\"on blue\", expand=False)\ndocs/source/progress.rst:22:For basic usage call the :func:`~rich.progress.track` function, which accepts a sequence (such as a list or range object) and an optional description of the job you are working on. The track function will yield values from the sequence and update the progress information on each iteration. Here's an example::\ndocs/source/progress.rst:126:The progress bar(s) will use only as much of the width of the terminal as required to show the task information. If you set the ``expand`` argument on the Progress constructor, then Rich will stretch the progress display to the full available width.\ndocs/source/progress.rst:182:    text_column = TextColumn(\"{task.description}\", table_column=Column(ratio=1))\ndocs/source/progress.rst:183:    bar_column = BarColumn(bar_width=None, table_column=Column(ratio=2))\ndocs/source/progress.rst:184:    progress = Progress(text_column, bar_column, expand=True)\ndocs/source/panel.rst:12:Panels will extend to the full width of the terminal. You can make panel *fit* the content by setting ``expand=False`` on the constructor, or by creating the Panel with :meth:`~rich.panel.Panel.fit`. For example::\ndocs/source/tree.rst:6:Run the following command to see a demonstration of a Rich tree::\ndocs/source/tree.rst:44:For a more practical demonstration, see `tree.py <https://github.com/willmcgugan/rich/blob/master/examples/tree.py>`_ which can generate a tree view of a directory in your hard drive.\ndocs/source/console.rst:173:You can specify how Rich should handle overflow with the ``overflow`` argument to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` which should be one of the following strings: \"fold\", \"crop\", \"ellipsis\", or \"ignore\". The default is \"fold\" which will put any excess characters on the following line, creating as many new lines as required to fit the text.\ndocs/source/console.rst:175:The \"crop\" method truncates the text at the end of the line, discarding any characters that would overflow.\ndocs/source/console.rst:179:The following code demonstrates the basic overflow methods::\ndocs/source/console.rst:187:    overflow_methods: List[OverflowMethod] = [\"fold\", \"crop\", \"ellipsis\"]\ndocs/source/console.rst:188:    for overflow in overflow_methods:\ndocs/source/console.rst:189:        console.rule(overflow)\ndocs/source/console.rst:190:        console.print(supercali, overflow=overflow, style=\"bold blue\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:210:You can also set overflow to \"ignore\" which allows text to run on to the next line. In practice this will look the same as \"crop\" unless you also set ``crop=False`` when calling :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print`.\ndocs/source/conf.py:1:# Configuration file for the Sphinx documentation builder.\ndocs/source/conf.py:5:# https://www.sphinx-doc.org/en/master/usage/configuration.html\ndocs/source/conf.py:35:# -- General configuration ---------------------------------------------------\ndocs/source/layout.rst:47:    <span style=\"color: #000080\">│</span>          <span style=\"font-weight: bold\">{</span><span style=\"color: #008000\">'size'</span>: <span style=\"color: #800080; font-style: italic\">None</span>, <span style=\"color: #008000\">'minimum_size'</span>: <span style=\"color: #000080; font-weight: bold\">1</span>, <span style=\"color: #008000\">'ratio'</span>: <span style=\"color: #000080; font-weight: bold\">1</span>, <span style=\"color: #008000\">'name'</span>: <span style=\"color: #008000\">'upper'</span><span style=\"font-weight: bold\">}</span>          <span style=\"color: #000080\">│</span>\ndocs/source/layout.rst:61:    <span style=\"color: #000080\">│</span>             <span style=\"color: #008000\">'ratio'</span>: <span style=\"color: #000080; font-weight: bold\">1</span>,                <span style=\"color: #000080\">││</span>             <span style=\"color: #008000\">'ratio'</span>: <span style=\"color: #000080; font-weight: bold\">1</span>,                <span style=\"color: #000080\">│</span>\ndocs/source/layout.rst:104:In addition to a fixed size, you can also make a flexible layout setting the ``ratio`` argument on the constructor or by assigning to the attribute. The ratio defines how much of the screen the layout should occupy in relation to other layouts. For example, let's reset the size and set the ratio of the upper layout to 2::\ndocs/source/layout.rst:107:    layout[\"upper\"].ratio = 2\ndocs/source/layout.rst:110:This makes the top layout take up two thirds of the space. This is because the default ratio is 1, giving the upper and lower layouts a combined total of 3. As the upper layout has a ratio of 2, it takes up two thirds of the space, leaving the remaining third for the lower layout.\ndocs/source/layout.rst:112:A layout with a ratio set may also have a minimum size to prevent it from getting too small. For instance, here's how we could set the minimum size of the lower sub-layout so that it won't shrink beyond 10 rows::\ndocs/source/layout.rst:124:The top layout is now invisible, and the \"lower\" layout will expand to fill the available space. Set ``visible`` to True to bring it back::\ndocs/source/layout.rst:129:You could use this to toggle parts of your interface based on your application's configuration.\ndocs/source/live.rst:8:For a demonstration of a live display, run the following command::\ndocs/source/live.rst:19:To create a live display, construct a :class:`~rich.live.Live` object with a renderable and use it as a context manager. The live display will persist for the duration of the context. You can update the renderable to update the display::\ndocs/source/live.rst:94:Vertical overflow\ndocs/source/live.rst:98:``vertical_overflow`` argument on the :class:`~rich.live.Live` constructor.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
